  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nAdditional chances of rain and storms exist through Friday night:\n\nMedium-range models have the frontal boundary and broad trough \nlingering in the same area through Friday night. Shortwaves that\nmove through the broad trough, along with surface convergence\nalong the front, will promote additional chances for showers and\nthunderstorms through Friday night.\n\nAt the moment, 15-25% chances for isolated activity exists\nThursday afternoon. There is currently no SPC risk for Thursday\nafternoon, however, given the proximity of the surface boundary,\nand environment characterized by 2500+ J/kg MUCAPE and 30-35 kts\nof 0-6 km bulk shear, would not be surprised to see another\nMarginal Risk come about.\n\nCoverage of showers and storms then look to increase again\nThursday night (40-70% chance) as the same pattern sets up with\na nocturnal LLJ/WAA and shortwave support. Depending on where\nthe frontal boundary is at this point in time, the Flood Watch\nmay need to be expanded and/or extended to account for\nadditional training and excessive rainfall potential. There is\ntoo much uncertainty at this time, though, to delineate any\nareas with enhanced risk.\n\nThe same pattern then occurs Friday and into friday night with\nisolated to scattered activity during the day, and focused more\nnumerous coverage along the front overnight. Once again, we will\nneed to monitor trends for any potential in heavy rainfall\nand/or strong to severe thunderstorms.\n\n\nExtended period of heat build this weekend and into next week:\n\nThe longwave trough axis finally moves east of our region\nSaturday and Sunday, making way for rising heights and\nincreasing temperatures. Highs over the weekend and into next\nweek are expected to be in the 90s, some areas approaching 100 \nF. Current dewpoint forecasts keep heat a tad lower than \n\"excessive\" with LREF ensembles showing <10% chance for >105 F \nHeat Index values. However, they do show >60% chances for >100 F\nvalues Saturday through at least Tuesday. So, while around 100 \nF Heat Index is not terribly uncommon for Ozarkian summers, the \nexpected extended nature of 100-105 F Heat Index values \nintroduces a Heat Risk as there will not be much room for heat \nrelief.\n\nWhile heat will build, the jet stream across the northern CONUS\nwill remain active. Some global models have the enhanced winds\ndropping south near our northern CWA. This may bring additional\nchances for rain and storms, but uncertainty in these types of\npatterns is too high for any appreciable mention above 15%. As\nan example, the EFI does show values of around 0.8 for CAPE-\nShear combo in central and northern Missouri, which may\nhighlight some potential for storms to come careening down the\nnorthwest flow of the jet stream sometime next week. Trends will\ncontinue to be monitored.",
